(VOA Chinese) – On Friday (February 10), the Biden administration blacklisted six Chinese entities suspected of involvement in the spy balloon program.
The U.S. Department of Commerce said the five Chinese companies and one research institution helped modernize the Chinese military, especially in the aviation and aerospace projects, including flying boats and aircraft.
Companies on the blacklist have difficulty obtaining US technology exports.
The list is called an “entity list,” and companies that enter it will be unable to purchase technology or goods from US suppliers unless they obtain a special export license from the US Department of Commerce.
The companies listed on the blacklist are: Beijing Nanjiang Sky Space Technology Co., Ltd., China Electronics Science and Technology Group Fourth Eighth Research Institute, Dongguan Lingkong Remote Sensing Technology Co., Ltd., Eagle Gate Aviation Technology Group Co., Ltd., Guangzhou Tianhai Xiang Aeronautical Technology Co., Ltd., and Shanxi Eagle Gate Aviation Technology Group Co., Ltd.
Prior to this, the White House had indicated that it was considering broader measures to “expose and address” China’s larger-scale surveillance activities that threatened US national security.
The balloon incident has further strained relations between China and the United States, and US Secretary of State Blinken has canceled his visit to China.
China's government expressed its solemn representations to the US embassy in China on February 4 after the US shot down the balloon, criticizing the US for seriously damaging and undermining the efforts and progress of stabilizing China-US relations.
Based on the existing debris evidence and information from the Chinese military procurement platform, the US government concluded that the manufacturer of the balloon was "directly related to the Chinese People's Liberation Army" and was an approved supplier of the Chinese military.
At a press conference on Friday, Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Mao Ning said that China has repeatedly explained the situation and clarified its position on the accidental entry of a Chinese aircraft without a pilot into United States airspace due to force majeure.
According to the State Department website, she added that the United States Congress resolution in question was purely political manipulation and hype.
China is strongly dissatisfied and firmly opposed to this.
Axelrod, an Assistant Secretary of Commerce for Export Enforcement at the US Department of Commerce, said that "Today's action demonstrates our efforts to identify and prevent China from using spy balloons, which have violated airspace over 40 countries including the United States."
Translation requested.
Reuters reported that an employee of Guangzhou Tianhai Chang Air Technology Co., Ltd. said he was unaware of the sanctions and refused to comment further.
China's embassy in the United States and the Chinese Foreign Ministry have not commented on the information.
(Reuters etc.)
