Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
The Singapore Tourism Board recently announced that it expects 12-14 million international visitors to Singapore in 2023, generating around S$18-21 billion in tourism revenue; that both number of visitors and tourism revenue will return to about 67-75% of pre-COVID-19 levels in 2019; and that the tourism industry is likely to fully recover in 2024.
The Singapore tourism market is gradually restarted and recovered in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board showed that the number of tourists entering the country in 2022 increased 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million, exceeding the earlier forecast of 4 million to 6 million, and recovered to 33% of the pre-pandemic level.
Tourism revenue in Singapore for January to September 2022 was S$8.96 billion, and it is expected that tourism revenue for the full year of 2022 will be between S$13.8 billion and S$14.3 billion, up sixfold to sevenfold from S$1.89 billion in 2021, which has already recovered to around 50% to 52% of pre-COVID-19 levels.
The Government of Singapore has taken a number of measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism sector.
In April 2022, the government announced an allocation of nearly 500 million Singapore dollars to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The Tourism Board announced earlier this month that it would allocate RM110 million from the budget over the next two years to attract more business and leisure activities to lure tourists back.
To make health and wellness a tourist attraction in Singapore, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an expression of interest exercise on 23 November 2022 to develop a health and wellness site along the southern coast of Singapore.
Wang Ling Li, Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put physical health at the top of their list of considerations.
The development of a health and lifestyle attraction in the South Coastal Zone, with its Bay Gardens and coastal embankment, is expected to contribute to Singapore &apos; s tourism and economy by encouraging tourists to consume and stay longer in Singapore.
The Director of the Singapore Tourism Authority, Chen Jianlong, said that health and wellness would become an important part of Singapore &apos; s tourism product in the coming years.
The COVID-19 pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this reason, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists for the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, providing more than 130 different types of health activities and experiences for the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences for visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children &apos; s Museum, the Avatar World at the Marina Bay Gardens, the Central Beach Marketplace at the Santa Monica Beach, the new &quot; Explore New Frontiers &quot; exhibition hall at the Museum of Art and Science and the new open-air theatre at the Night Wildlife Park, the Tourism Board will support the development and renewal of projects such as the Bird Park at the World of Birds, which will make Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
Data show that as of December 18, 2022, passenger traffic at Changi Airport has recovered strongly, with up to 150,000 passengers served daily and about one million passengers per week, which is about 75% of the pre-COVID level, and the number of airline employees has returned to about 90% of pre-pandemic levels.
Data showed that from January to October this year, Singapore received 23.6 million tourists, which is equivalent to 42% of the pre-epidemic level, and completed 170,000 flights, which is equivalent to 53% of the pre-epidemic level.
The tourism industry is recovering quickly, which is also driving the recovery of accommodation, transportation, dining and shopping industries.
The Singapore government and tourism industry are placing high hopes in the Chinese market.
Khoo Suet-Dar, Assistant Director of the International Department of the Singapore Tourism Board, said that a survey of China's tourism platform showed that Singapore's safety index ranked among the top three in the world, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular among Chinese tourists.
Before the pandemic, China welcomed about 3 million visitors to Singapore for travel and tourism every year.
With China easing border policies, it is expected that this year’s number of inbound tourists will recover to 30-60% of the level in 2019.
Ko Su-kyung said that the Tourism Board will now closely monitor further signals released by China on outbound travel policies to respond to changing tourist demands.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies such as China Travel, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the emerging trends in traveling abroad.
Looking ahead to 2023, Singapore Tourism Board is optimistic about the tourism market, believing that as long as global economic conditions do not experience significant downturns and there are no new large-scale outbreaks of deadly pandemics, the pace of tourism recovery will be faster than expected.
