The Lonely Lion
When I got the book, I thought it would be as simple as The Lost Piece of Paper, but turns out there's still a lot of text!
It took three reading sessions to finish the book for the children, a little lion who can shoot, a little lion who loves to eat fruit juice and sweets.
The scene of the lion eating the hunter is a bit uncomfortable, and the arrangement of being eaten by animals is a bit unacceptable to modern urban people.
In the end, this little lion who succeeded in human society has lost himself; he is no longer entirely a lion nor an entirely human being.
Finally, only the lion's back is left, and we don't know where he went.
Perhaps years later, the image of the little lion will remain in the child &apos; s mind as she grows up, recalled at an unexpected moment one day, and perhaps touched or inspired her.
